One more consideration: the statePublish block catches ALL exceptions in `catch (ePub)` and just warns — so a broken implementation might not fail the tick but simply warn. My tests assert on the published snapshot payload directly, so a missing fetch fails the assertion. Good.

How do I detect "the published snapshot"? The PUT command: `gh api -X PUT repos/o/r/contents/data/fa-state.json -f branch=factory-data ... -f content="$(printf %s '<json>' | base64)"`. updateHistory also PUTs `data/fa-state-<stamp>.json` and `data/fa-state-history.json`. I'll filter commands matching `contents/data/fa-state.json ` (exact asset, the first PUT) — use regex `/X PUT repos\/o\/r\/contents\/data\/fa-state\.json\b/` — careful: `fa-state-2026...json` also contains `fa-state`... `data/fa-state.json` with the `.json` right after `fa-state` distinguishes from `fa-state-20260923-2235.json`. And history is `fa-state-history.json`. So matching `-X PUT repos/o/r/contents/data/fa-state.json ` works (there's a space after the path before `-f branch=`).

